Super Mario Bros. Meta-Disassembly

This repository consists of a single code-base disassembly of
four flavors of the original Super Mario Bros. engine
as developed by SRD:

- Super Mario Bros.
- Super Mario Bros. (Famicom Disk System Reissue)
- Super Mario Bros. 2
- All Night Nippon Super Mario Bros.

These represent the four released specimens of this
particular engine for Nintendo's 8-bit home console.

The utilities required to compile the FDS image are included
as a submodule of this repository.
Otherwise this repository should build on UNIX-like platforms
using the cc65 6502 development suite.

Throughout the disassembly, preprocessor directives are
used to section off code that differs between these
various versions, such that providing the correct combination
of these definitions will produce one of the titles
in question.  Note, a complete title cannot be produced without
at least some subset of these definitions.  As such,
the provided Makefile includes the definitions required
to build Super Mario Bros. for the Famicom.

Some such directives are not specific to releases
but rather the game or console environment involved, such
that inclusion of just those defines, ideally, would produce
a game with those desired features synthesized from
the various streams.

To extract the necessary assets from each title, extraction
scripts are provided expecting either an iNES image in the
case of Super Mario Bros. or an FDS image in the case of the
remaining titles.  The SHA1 sums for the reference
specimens are:

Super Mario Bros. (FC)  - ea343f4e445a9050d4b4fbac2c77d0693b1d0922
Super Mario Bros. (FDS) - 407cbbacf998dd2a644b1d93aec435555909affd
Super Mario Bros. 2     - 3b8c8998b4887d6dd676965943d69a320738ab9c
ANN Super Mario Bros.   - 66cec5e6501d9f4c35bac8093d287d6ce04b561c

If your FDS images begin not "FDS" but something like "*NINTENDO-HVC*"
then you need to add a 16-byte header first.

Some scripts overwrite the results of other scripts.  Where this
happens, this is due to dulicate data across titles, running any
individual script should produce all assets required to rebuild
that particular title.

This disassembly does *not* claim to produce 1:1 FDS images
compared with those passed through the extraction scripts.
Rather, the files contained within the FDS images should be
1:1 copies whereas the FDS image will reflect local filenames
and timestamps.  In other words, the disk image is fully functional,
but the disk superlabel may not compare 1:1 with a disk pressed
in the factory in the 80s, particularly regarding the creation
date and some file naming details.

This will take some time...
